// Analytics build configuration — COMMITTED, and credential-free by design.
//
// Every value below is empty on purpose. A contributor clone and a fork build with empty
// analytics credentials, `AppInfo.telemetryDeckAppID` resolves to nil, and the analytics client
// no-ops entirely — so an ordinary build cannot reach the owner's dataset. Keeping the dataset
// clean is the whole point; the app ID is a write-only ingestion key and is not a secret in any
// case.
//
// Two things supply the real values, and neither is committed:
//
//   * Local development — `Config/Analytics.local.xcconfig`, gitignored and optionally included
//     at the bottom of this file. Order matters — a later assignment wins, so the empty defaults
//     must come first and the optional include last.
//   * Release CI — `.github/workflows/deploy.yml` and `deploy-pre-release.yml` pass both values
//     to `xcodebuild archive` as command-line build settings, sourced from repository secrets.
//     Command-line settings outrank an xcconfig, so they win over the empty defaults below
//     without any local file existing. Each workflow then reads the archived Info.plist back and
//     fails the build if either key came out empty.
//
// So a release IS credentialed, and the runtime opt-out — not the absence of this file — is what
// an installed release's users control.

// TelemetryDeck's write-only ingestion key. Absent here; supplied by the local override.
TELEMETRYDECK_APP_ID =

// The identifier salt. This value is write-once: changing it after a release re-derives every
// anonymized identifier, so every existing install looks new to the vendor and all retention
// and DAU/MAU history resets with no way to reconcile the two sides afterwards.
TELEMETRYDECK_SALT =

// Optional include — the trailing `?` makes an absent file a silent no-op rather than an error,
// which is what lets a clean clone build without a configuration-file warning.
#include? "Analytics.local.xcconfig"
